        <div id="five-ways" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Five Ways to Make a Concrete Driveway Black
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every black concrete driveway gets its colour in one of two places: inside the concrete, or on top of it. That single distinction predicts nearly everything about how the finish behaves over the next ten years. Colour that lives inside the slab cannot peel and wears only as fast as the concrete itself wears. Colour that lives in a film on the surface is easier to get perfectly black and perfectly even, but it wears in the tire lanes, scuffs where wheels turn, and has to be renewed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Integral colour is mixed into the truck before the pour, so it is only an option for new concrete or a replacement slab. Acid stain and water-based stain soak into an existing slab and colour the top layer of the paste. Dyes do the same with much finer colourants. Tinted sealers carry pigment in the sealer film, and solid colour stains and concrete paints are opaque coatings that hide the grey completely.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Method</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Where the colour lives</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">How black it gets</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Main weakness</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Integral colour</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Through the full slab</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Charcoal to near-black</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">New pours only; shows efflorescence</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Acid stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Top layer of paste</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mottled brown-black</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unpredictable on old or dense slabs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water-based stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Top layer of paste</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Even, semi-transparent black</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Relies on sealer for longevity</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tinted sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">In the sealer film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Deep, uniform</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wears in tire lanes; patchy recoats</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solid stain or paint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Opaque surface film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">True jet black</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Peels and scuffs; recoat cycle</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For an existing driveway, the realistic choices are a water-based stain under a good sealer, or a solid colour coating. Acid stain can work but rarely gives a true black. For a new driveway, integral colour is the most durable route, with a stain or tinted sealer layered on top if the integral shade alone is not dark enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before choosing, it is worth being honest about why you want black. If the aim is to hide oil drips and tire grime on a busy household driveway, almost any dark method succeeds. If the aim is a crisp, uniform, architectural black that stays crisp, only the film-forming options get there, and they demand the most upkeep. Most disappointment with black driveways comes from expecting the second result from a method designed for the first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="integral" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Integral Colour: Black Mixed In at the Pour
          </h2>

          <p>
            Integral colour means adding pigment to the concrete mix itself, so the whole slab is coloured through. Chips, wear and saw cuts all show the same colour as the surface. It is the closest thing to a permanent black concrete driveway, and the only method with no coating to renew for the colour to survive.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Iron oxide or carbon black</h3>

          <p>
            There are two families of black pigment. Black iron oxide is the most commonly used, according to Davis Colors, and it binds into the cement paste as a permanent part of the concrete. Its limitation is depth: it produces a dark charcoal rather than a true black, and its tone leans slightly brown. Carbon black gives a deeper, bluer black and has roughly two to five times the tinting strength, so less is needed. The catch, as industry sources including Davis Colors acknowledge, is that carbon black is not locked into the cement matrix the same way and can weather out of exterior concrete over time. One pigment maker quoted in the trade press goes further and says it should never be used outdoors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Carbon black also interferes with air entrainment, which matters a great deal for a driveway in a freeze-thaw climate. Entrained air is what lets concrete survive repeated freezing; a Portland Cement Association engineer has noted that adding carbon black reduces air content, so the admixture dose has to be raised and tested. Some liquid carbon black products are formulated to avoid this, but the simpler answer for an exterior slab in a cold region is iron oxide, accepting a charcoal rather than a jet black.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why integral black is never quite black</h3>

          <p>
            Ordinary grey cement limits how dark a pigment can push the mix, and pigment loading has a ceiling. ASTM C979, the standard for pigments in integrally coloured concrete, caps the dosage at 10 percent of the cement weight, and many products specify less. Beyond a certain point more pigment adds cost without adding colour. That is why a lot of so-called black driveways are integrally coloured charcoal with a stain or tinted sealer on top to finish the job.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other thing integral black exposes is efflorescence, the white mineral bloom that migrates to the surface of most new concrete. On a grey slab nobody notices it. On a black one it is the first thing anyone sees. It usually dry-brushes off and diminishes as the slab ages, but expect a patchy, whitish first season, and do not judge the colour until it has passed. If you are pricing a new slab, our guide to <a href="/concrete-driveway-cost/">concrete driveway cost</a> covers how coloured mixes are typically quoted.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Staining an Existing Driveway Black
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a driveway that is already poured, a black stained concrete driveway is the usual answer. Stain colours the top layer of the slab rather than covering it, so the broom texture, the aggregate and the character of the concrete still show through. That is the appeal, and also the limitation: stain is translucent, so every patch, oil ghost and colour difference in the original slab shows through too.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Acid stain: deep, mottled, rarely jet black</h3>

          <p>
            Reactive acid stains use metallic salts that react with the free lime in the concrete, so the colour becomes part of the surface and cannot peel. Kemiko's Stone Tone line includes a black, and the company's own description of the result is a mottled tortoise-shell black rather than a flat one. On a driveway, that variegation can look rich and expensive, or it can look blotchy, depending on the slab. Older driveways have often lost much of the free lime the reaction needs, so the colour may come up weak and uneven. A test patch carried all the way through to the sealer is not optional.
          </p>

          <p>
            Acid stain also brings handling overhead: acid-resistant sprayers, eye protection, careful masking of anything nearby, and a thorough neutralising rinse before any sealer goes down. Residual acid under a sealer is a common cause of sealer failure months later. If you want the step-by-step version of that process, our sibling guide on <a href="/how-to-stain-a-concrete-patio/">how to stain a concrete patio</a> walks through prep, application and neutralising on a slab where the stakes are lower.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water-based stain: predictable and even</h3>

          <p>
            Water-based penetrating stains deposit fine pigment into the pores without a chemical reaction. The colour you choose is close to the colour you get, which matters when the goal is black rather than brown. They work on newer concrete and on older slabs where acid stain struggles, and cleanup is water. Several makers sell a black specifically for exterior concrete, and most of them say plainly that the colour must be sealed to last. Our roundup of the <a href="/best-outdoor-concrete-stain/">best outdoor concrete stain</a> covers the products and how they behave after a season outdoors.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a driveway the honest expectation for either stain is a deep charcoal-black that reads as black from the street but shows some variation up close. The slab will also wear unevenly: the tire lanes take the abrasion, and if the sealer over them is allowed to wear through, grit starts to polish away the stained surface paste. That is why sealing is not a finishing touch on a stained driveway. It is the part that keeps the colour.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tinted-sealer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Concrete Dyes and Black Tinted Sealers
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dyes: strong colour, sun-sensitive</h3>

          <p>
            Concrete dyes use much finer colourants than stains, often in an acetone or water carrier, and they produce intense, even colour quickly. Traditionally they were treated as an interior product because many fade in direct sunlight. Some manufacturers now market UV-stable dyes for exterior use, but even those generally say a sealer is required to lock in the colour. On a driveway, a dye is best thought of as a colour boost under a sealer rather than a standalone finish, and you should only use one whose maker explicitly rates it for exterior exposure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tinted sealers: black in the film</h3>

          <p>
            A tinted sealer is a black concrete driveway sealer in the literal sense: a clear sealer with pigment added, so it colours and protects in one step. Some are sold pre-tinted, and some contractors add a tint pack to a solvent-based acrylic. The appeal is speed. A plain grey driveway can be dark charcoal by the afternoon, with no acid, no neutralising and no separate sealing step.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-off is that all of the colour is in a film perhaps a few thousandths of an inch thick. Wherever that film wears, the grey comes back. On a driveway, that means the tire lanes and the turning area lighten first, while the edges and the area under the car stay dark. Owners commonly report that a tinted acrylic on a driveway starts to look patchy within months in the high-wear zones. Recoating helps, but fresh tinted coats over worn areas rarely match the unworn areas exactly, so the patchwork can get worse rather than better.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tinted sealers work best as the final layer over a stained or integrally coloured slab, where the sealer adds depth and evens the colour but the concrete underneath is already dark. When the tint wears in the lanes, what shows through is dark charcoal rather than bright grey, and the wear is far less obvious. Used that way, a tinted sealer is one of the most useful tools for getting a black finish. Used alone over bare grey concrete on a driveway, it is a high-maintenance choice.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Put the colour as deep into the concrete as you can.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Every layer of black that lives in the slab rather than on it is a layer that cannot wear off in the tire lanes. Use integral colour or stain for the base, and treat tinted sealer as the top-up, not the foundation.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="solid-colour" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Solid Colour Stain and Concrete Paint
          </h2>

          <p>
            If what you want is a true, uniform jet black, an opaque coating is the only method that reliably gets there. Solid colour concrete stains and driveway paints cover the slab completely, hiding patches, mismatched pours and old oil ghosts that would show through a translucent stain. For a tired, blotchy driveway that no transparent product can rescue, this is often the right answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Product choice matters more here than with any other method, because many concrete paints are rated for patios and porches but not for vehicles. Look for a coating whose maker specifically rates it for vehicular traffic. Sherwin-Williams' H&amp;C Colortop Solvent-Based Solid Color Sealer 250, for example, is offered in a ready-mixed Black, carries a satin finish, and is listed by the maker as suitable for vehicular traffic on exterior horizontal surfaces. Our <a href="/best-driveway-paint-reviews/">driveway paint roundup</a> covers more options at this end of the category.
          </p>

          <p>
            The weaknesses are the weaknesses of any film. It can peel where moisture pushes up through the slab, it scuffs where tires turn, and a hot summer tire parked on a soft acrylic film can lift it. Every chip shows bright grey through jet black, which is the least forgiving contrast there is. Most solid colour finishes on a driveway want a fresh coat every few years in the traffic lanes, and the edges and untrafficked areas last longer, so recoats tend to be partial and need careful feathering.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also changes what the driveway is. A painted black driveway looks painted rather than like coloured concrete, and once a slab has been coated, going back to stain means stripping or grinding every trace of it first. If you are weighing the two approaches more broadly, our comparison of <a href="/concrete-stain-vs-paint/">concrete stain vs paint</a> sets out where each one makes sense and how each one fails.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical tip if you go opaque: buy enough for the whole driveway plus a recoat from one batch, and keep the leftover sealed and labelled. Touch-ups from a different batch, or from a different brand of black, rarely match, and on a black driveway the mismatch shows in strong sun.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="living-with" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Living With a Black Driveway Is Actually Like
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heat</h3>

          <p>
            Dark surfaces absorb more sunlight and run hotter. Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory's Heat Island Group puts new asphalt, the nearest everyday comparison to a black driveway, at about 5 percent solar reflectance, against 30 to 50 percent for cool pavements such as lighter concrete. In practical terms a black driveway gets noticeably hotter underfoot in summer, radiates more heat toward the house in the evening, and is harder on bare feet and pets. In hot climates that is a real cost. In cold ones it is partly a benefit, because a dark slab sheds light snow and ice faster on sunny days.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat also matters for any film on the surface. A hotter slab softens acrylic sealers and coatings more readily, which is part of why hot tire pickup and scuffing are more common on dark driveways than on grey ones. If you live somewhere with long, hot summers, lean toward stain or integral colour under a harder sealer rather than a soft tinted or painted film.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dirt, salt and residue</h3>

          <p>
            Black hides oil and grime well, which is why it is popular on working driveways. It hides almost nothing that is light-coloured. Dust, pollen, dried water spots, dried mud and especially road salt show up as pale haze. In winter, a black driveway can look grey-white with salt residue for weeks. Rinsing after storms helps, and so does choosing a de-icer that leaves less residue. Our guide to the <a href="/best-ice-melt-for-concrete/">best ice melt for concrete</a> covers which products are gentler on the surface.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tire marks and wear lanes</h3>

          <p>
            Tire marks behave differently depending on the method. On integral colour or stain under a penetrating sealer, there is no soft film for tires to scuff, so marks are mostly rubber deposits that clean off. On film-forming sealers and paints, power-steering scuffs at the turning point and hot tire marks where cars park are the most common complaint. The traffic lanes also lose sheen and colour first, so a glossy black driveway tends to develop two matte stripes over time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fading is the last item, and it applies to everything except iron oxide integral colour. Stains under a worn sealer lighten, tinted films wear thin, dyes and carbon black can weather out, and paint chalks in strong sun. None of this is a reason not to do it. It is a reason to plan the maintenance before you start rather than discovering it in year two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sealing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Sealing a Black Driveway and the Resealing Cycle
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every route to a black concrete driveway ends with a sealer, and the sealer decides how long the colour lasts. The choice is between a penetrating sealer, which soaks in and leaves no film, and a film-forming acrylic or urethane, which deepens colour and adds sheen. Our guide on <a href="/acrylic-vs-penetrating-concrete-sealer/">acrylic vs penetrating concrete sealer</a> goes into the chemistry; for a black driveway the practical difference comes down to looks against upkeep.
          </p>

          <p>
            A film-forming sealer makes black look blacker. Most clear acrylics darken stained concrete by a shade or more and add a wet or satin sheen, which is usually what people picture when they imagine a black driveway. The cost is a film that wears in the tire lanes, can whiten if moisture gets trapped, and needs renewing, commonly every two to three years on a driveway. A penetrating silane or siloxane leaves the colour looking more matte and natural, does not scuff or peel, and protects against water and salt, but it does less for depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            A sensible combination for many stained driveways is a penetrating sealer where cars drive and park, and a film-forming sealer only where you want the visual depth and there is little traffic. If you want the wet look everywhere, choose a product the maker rates for hot tire resistance; our reviews of the <a href="/best-concrete-driveway-sealer-reviews/">best concrete driveway sealers</a> flag which ones are urethane-modified for exactly that reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing matters as much as product. Seal only once stain is fully dry and, for acid stain, fully neutralised and rinsed. Seal on a dry day within the label's temperature window, in two thin coats rather than one heavy one, because a thick coat of acrylic over a dark slab is the classic recipe for cloudy, whitened patches that show up starkly on black. Our step-by-step on <a href="/how-to-seal-a-concrete-driveway/">how to seal a concrete driveway</a> covers the prep and application in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            After that, watch the water test. When water stops beading on the tire lanes and starts darkening the surface, the sealer is worn there and it is time to recoat, ideally before grit gets to the stain beneath. Resealing a sound surface every few years is a short job. Restaining a driveway whose colour has been worn off in the lanes is a much bigger one, and the new colour rarely matches the old edges exactly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting Integral Colour Alone to Be Jet Black</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Grey cement and the ASTM C979 dosage ceiling limit how dark a mix can go, so integrally coloured driveways usually come out charcoal. If you want true black, plan from the start to finish with a black stain or tinted sealer over the integral colour, and test the combination on a sample slab.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Carbon Black Pigment in a Freezing Climate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Carbon black can reduce entrained air, which is what protects concrete from freeze-thaw damage, and it can weather out of exterior concrete. Unless the supplier specifically formulates and tests it for this, choose black iron oxide for an outdoor slab in a cold region and accept a slightly softer black.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a Tinted Sealer Straight on Grey Concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                All the colour lives in a thin film, so the tire lanes wear back to bright grey first and recoats rarely match the unworn edges. Tinted sealer works far better as a top layer over a stained or integrally coloured slab, where wear exposes charcoal rather than grey.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting With a Coating Not Rated for Vehicles</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many concrete paints are made for patios and porches. On a driveway they scuff, lift under hot tires and peel within a season. Check that the maker explicitly rates the product for vehicular traffic before buying, and follow its prep requirements to the letter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Full Test Patch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Black stains, dyes and tinted sealers all look different once sealed, and acid stain varies slab to slab. Test a square yard in an inconspicuous corner, take it all the way through to the final sealer, and look at it in full sun before committing to the whole driveway.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you make a concrete driveway black?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a new driveway, add black iron oxide pigment to the mix and finish with a black stain or tinted sealer if you want it darker. On an existing driveway, use a black water-based or acid stain sealed with a driveway-rated sealer, or an opaque solid colour coating rated for vehicles if you want a uniform jet black.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a black concrete driveway fade?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most methods do to some extent. Iron oxide integral colour is the most permanent. Stains hold well while the sealer over them is maintained, but lighten in the tire lanes once it wears. Tinted sealers, paints, dyes and carbon black pigment fade or wear fastest and need the most frequent renewal.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a black concrete driveway hotter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Dark surfaces reflect far less sunlight; Berkeley Lab puts new asphalt at about 5 percent solar reflectance against 30 to 50 percent for cool pavements. A black slab gets hotter underfoot and radiates more heat in the evening, though it also sheds light snow and ice faster on sunny winter days.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best black concrete driveway sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on what is underneath. Over stain or integral colour, a clear sealer rated for driveways protects the colour; a urethane-modified acrylic adds depth and resists hot tires. A tinted black sealer adds colour too, but on bare grey concrete it wears in the tire lanes, so use it as a top layer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you acid stain a driveway black?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but expect a mottled, brown-black finish rather than a flat jet black. Kemiko describes its black acid stain result as a mottled tortoise-shell black. Results depend on the slab's free lime, so older or very dense driveways may take colour weakly. Always test a patch through to the sealer first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a black driveway show dirt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It hides oil and tire grime well but shows anything light-coloured: dust, pollen, dried water spots, efflorescence and especially road salt. In winter a black driveway can look grey-white with salt residue until it is rinsed. Regular hosing and a low-residue de-icer make a noticeable difference.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often does a black stained concrete driveway need resealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A film-forming sealer on a driveway commonly needs renewing every two to three years, sooner in the tire lanes. Penetrating sealers last longer but add less depth. Use the water test: when water stops beading in the traffic areas and darkens the surface, reseal before grit wears the stained surface beneath.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

